<ProseBlock
  heading="Putting it to work"
  layout="stacked"
  image={mockData}
  imageAlt="Three kinds of mock data for the prototypes: Users, with persona info as mock user profiles that are switchable in-app; Businesses, by business category, such as retail versus a local restaurant; and Assets, context-specific complete sets of images per business, prompted with skill files"
  imageCaption="Mock data, dynamically served."
>
  <p>
    With all of this in place, I used the kit to prototype the flows for our
    upcoming Business Home launch. (The product itself is its own story: see the <a
      href={businessHomeHref}
      data-overlay-target={CASE_STUDY_IDS.businessHome}
      >Business Home case study</a
    >.) The difference showed in the details. My prototypes used only components
    and tokens from the new design library. In other prototypes, agents kept
    pulling in older Material Design 3 components, which left them off-brand.
  </p>
  <p>
    With the basics handled, I had room to push on interaction. I explored
    ambient effects for a loading interstitial, the page people see while gen AI
    does its work. I also tried a more interactive effect for the same page. We
    considered it and decided not to use it, but it showed how far you can push
    interaction in an effect. I shared both with the team as inspiration.
  </p>
</ProseBlock>

