<ProseBlock heading="Impact">
  <p>
    I wasn’t in a position to steer the shared sandbox, but the kit made an
    impression on the people closest to the work. The two designers I worked
    with directly tried it, and they came to me for tips and troubleshooting as
    they built their own prototypes. The sandbox team applied some of the tips
    I’d shared.
  </p>
  <p>
    Word spread beyond our team. My Google point of contact asked me to walk a
    designer from another team through the kit, to help her think through how
    she could use AI prototyping. We also showed it to our engineers, who were
    struggling with fidelity and quick turnarounds. One of them joked, “You’re
    doing more engineering than I am!” At the end of my contract, I handed the
    kit to my teammates.
  </p>
  <DraftNote label="Decide">
    <p>Engineer quote: keep or cut?</p>
  </DraftNote>
  <p>
    I also made some tradeoffs on purpose. I built a separate kit instead of
    waiting for access to production code, so it could drift from what
    engineering shipped. And by ruling out third-party libraries and raw values,
    I traded some flexibility for consistency.
  </p>
  <DraftNote label="Confirm">
    <p>Are these the tradeoffs you’d name?</p>
  </DraftNote>
  <DraftNote label="Footer line">
    <p>
      To me, this is the good side of AI in design: it didn’t replace design
      judgment, it let me carry it further. (Your outline only said this showed
      AI’s positive potential; this wording is Claude’s, so keep it only if it
      sounds like you.)
    </p>
  </DraftNote>
</ProseBlock>
